From fdb722c61b83e4ee5ff768a159b879b1966d1497 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zenobit <zen@osowoso.org>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 23:23:50 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] render: blit decoded sixel images onto the display

Terminal emulation and sixel decoding live in libtsm; this wires the
decoded pixels through to the framebuffer.

The only pixel primitive kmscon had was display_blend(), which takes an
8-bit alpha mask and interpolates a single ink colour against the cell
background -- fine for a glyph, useless for an image that carries its own
colours. Added display_blit_rgba() and struct video_rgba_blit_req, a
full-colour RGBA source blitted with its own alpha, implemented for the
drm2d and fbdev backends. Both follow the same bounds handling as
display_blend(): requests are clipped to the display, a rectangle starting
outside it is rejected, and the source advances by its own row pitch so a
clipped blit still reads the right pixels. fbdev additionally handles the
16/24bpp framebuffer modes, where it converts through xrgb32_to_device()
and treats alpha as a 50% threshold rather than reading back and
re-blending a pixel it has not decoded.

kmscon_text_ops gains an optional draw_image, which kmscon_text_draw_images()
fills from libtsm's tsm_screen_images_draw(). It is called from
do_redraw_screen() between the glyph pass and the render pass, so images
are re-blitted every repaint and survive resizes. Backends without the op
(gltex) simply report -EOPNOTSUPP and are unaffected.

Fixes the cells an image covers.

Damage tracking in bbulk is cell-based: bbulk_compute_damage() turns the
per-cell damage flags into rectangles and hands them to display_set_damage(),
which DRM2D passes to the display controller as the plane's FB_DAMAGE_CLIPS.
An image changes pixels without changing any cell's text, so bbulk_draw()
never marked those cells and the image's rectangle never reached damage --
meaning on any display whose primary plane advertises FB_DAMAGE_CLIPS (the
common case for atomic modesetting drivers) the image simply would not
appear. bbulk_draw_image() now marks the cells it covers, clamped to the
grid, so the existing damage pass picks them up.

PTYs now report pixel geometry.

Graphics applications determine a cell's pixel size by asking the terminal,
falling back in order to XTSMGRAPHICS, CSI 14 t, and finally the TIOCGWINSZ
pixel fields -- and kmscon was zeroing ws_xpixel/ws_ypixel along with the
rest of struct winsize, so the last fallback yielded a zero-sized viewport.
kmscon_pty_open() and kmscon_pty_resize() take the cell pixel size and fill
both fields with clamped products. The cell size comes from the first
enabled text renderer, since a pty has a single pixel geometry and that
renderer's font reflects per-screen scaling better than the terminal's
default. A null font falls back to zero, preserving the old behaviour.

libcheck is now optional. It was required whenever -Dtests=true, so a tree
without it failed at configure time and ran no tests at all. Only test_shl
uses it, so it is now guarded and the rest of the suite builds and runs
anywhere. tests/test_bbulk.c gains coverage for the image damage path,
including an image hanging off the bottom-right corner and one placed
entirely past the grid.

The GL renderer is still unsupported for images, and images are placed
unrotated, so they are mis-oriented rather than corrupt on a rotated
display.
